course: dictionary
lesson: 10742
title: Upthrust: the push that holds things up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When you put an object in water, the water pushes up on it. This upward push is called upthrust. «Water pushes up on anything placed in it; this push is called upthrust.» That is why a bath toy feels lighter when you lift it out of the water.
explain: Upthrust helps decide whether something floats or sinks. «An object floats when the upthrust is equal to its weight.» Upthrust pushes up, and weight pulls down. If the weight is bigger, the object sinks.
explain: Geographers use the word too, but for rock. Upthrust can mean land or rock being pushed upward, for example when mountains slowly rise. Read the words around it: if the sentence is about water, boats and floating, it means the science meaning; if it is about land and mountains, it means the rock meaning.
fact: Upthrust is an upward push from a liquid or a gas.
fact: «Things feel lighter in water because the upthrust holds up part of their weight.»
fact: «Salt water gives more upthrust than fresh water.»
fact: Upthrust is often drawn as an arrow pointing up on a force diagram.
fact: In geography, upthrust means rock or land being pushed upward, as when mountains slowly rise.
exercise: «Things feel lighter in water because the upthrust holds up part of their weight.» What is upthrust? | Rock that sinks under the sea | A kind of fish that lives in salt water | A heavy pull that drags things down to the bottom | *An upward push from water on an object placed in it
exercise: «An object floats when the upthrust is equal to its weight.» Based on the lesson, what happens if the weight is bigger than the upthrust? | *It sinks | It turns into rock | It becomes lighter than before | It floats high on top of the water
exercise: «Salt water gives more upthrust than fresh water.» Which water would push up harder on a boat? | Fresh water | *Salt water | Both push exactly the same | Neither pushes at all
test: «Water pushes up on anything placed in it; this push is called upthrust.» What is the upward push of water on an object called? | Density | Gravity | Weight | *Upthrust
test: In geography, what can upthrust mean? | Water flowing down a hill | A bird landing on a cliff | *Rock or land pushed upward slowly, such as when mountains rise | A strong wind blowing across the sea
exam: When a boat floats, how does the upthrust on it compare with its weight? | *Equal to its weight | Much smaller than its weight | Zero, because it is in water | Always bigger than its weight, so it rises
